# LINE ENDINGS, DECIDED HERE RATHER THAN BY WHOEVER CLONED IT.
#
# Git for Windows sets `core.autocrlf=true` at install time, so a Windows
# checkout of a repository with no `.gitattributes` gets CRLF in every text
# file — including the 89 authored YAML files, the schemas the editor generates
# its forms from, and `docs/VOCABULARY.md`, which `docs.test.ts` compares
# against freshly rendered output. `gen-docs.ts` already strips `\r\n` before
# that comparison, which is the shape of a bug being worked around one file at
# a time: the next test to read a file byte for byte finds out on somebody
# else's machine, and only on somebody else's machine.
#
# `eol=lf` says the working tree gets LF on every platform. Nothing here needs
# CRLF on disk — Node, vitest, vite and every editor anybody uses read LF
# natively on Windows — except the one file that is interpreted by the Windows
# command processor.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Interpreted by cmd.exe, which is the one reader in this repository that has
# ever cared. Gradle ships it CRLF and it stays that way.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Read by `sh` on the runner, so LF is not a preference there — a CRLF
# `gradlew` fails with a `\r: command not found` that names no file.
gradlew text eol=lf

# Never touched, never diffed, never merged.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jar  binary
*.aab  binary
*.apk  binary
*.keystore binary
